<p>Run Claude Code in a folder holding three meeting-note files and ask for this.</p>
<pre><code>Combine these three meeting notes and summarize them into a single report. In Korean.</code></pre>
<p>The key point here is that <strong>you never told it which files exist</strong>. With web chat you would have to upload the files yourself, but Claude Code</p>
<ul>
    <li>first <strong>explores (searches) which files are in the current folder</strong>,</li>
    <li><strong>reads those files one by one</strong>,</li>
    <li>and after <strong>analyzing</strong> the content, produces the result (the report).</li>
</ul>
<p>In other words, "the folder you launched it from" becomes the workspace, and it handles the files inside on its own.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Worth remembering</span>
    Claude Code has <strong>access to the entire folder</strong> in which you ran <code>claude</code>. That is why it matters to split folders per project and make a habit of launching inside the folder you want to work in.
</div>
